#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f38863b-74f6-56b0-9ede-fdc22d79153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370081} {:answer/id #uuid "65bdd1e2-201e-5a9a-bbd1-4af8aa33cddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370082} {:answer/id #uuid "73dd4000-e170-56cf-b0bf-be74a0168abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370083} {:answer/id #uuid "16906ed8-8aaa-5e95-9569-f7b9ad59835a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{5}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370084} {:answer/id #uuid "44eb18c9-10ed-5271-b2b0-5aa22cc230d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73dd4000-e170-56cf-b0bf-be74a0168abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370083} :answer-field/id #uuid "577af028-99a4-57d4-b6f6-ead9e4c91fc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55ba2de5-4728-586d-af2e-e9e7120ebc2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-330842" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}}} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
